Make the canonical scan matcher's pkg-config file name the shared library
rather than the static one.

The matcher's build produces two libraries from the same sources: a default
one, which is shared when the build is told to prefer shared libraries, and a
second that is forced static whatever it is told. Both are installed. Its
pkg-config file names the static one.

That matters here rather than being a preference. The matcher is LGPL v3, and
the relinking obligation that licence places on anybody redistributing a
binary is met by linking against it dynamically. A caller that asks
pkg-config how to link, which is the obvious thing to do and the reason the
file is shipped at all, is handed the static archive instead and links it in,
quietly, with nothing to say so. Reading the linker command afterwards is the
only way to find out. Naming the shared library removes the trap rather than
leaving it for somebody to fall into.

Nothing else about the file changes, and both libraries are still built and
still installed. Only the answer pkg-config gives is corrected.

This is the Eigen variant of the matcher, on the `csm_eigen` branch, pinned at
commit 04c4a31072e8690bd0ff14de50be20c5b6b43e66. The fault is upstream's
rather than this tree's, so it belongs in an upstream report; until one is
made and acted on, this patch is carried here.

Apply from the root of the matcher's own sources:

  patch -p1 < csm_pkgconfig_shared.patch

--- a/pkg-config/csm.pc.in
+++ b/pkg-config/csm.pc.in
@@ -5,5 +5,5 @@ includedir=${prefix}/include
 Name: csm
 Description: The C(anonical) Scan Matcher
 Version: @csm_version@
-Libs: -L${libdir} -lcsm-static 
+Libs: -L${libdir} -lcsm 
 Cflags: @PKG_CONFIG_C_FLAGS@ -I${includedir}
\ No newline at end of file
